CORE dfcc-only
main.c
--dfcc main --apply-loop-contracts _ --no-standard-checks
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
^\[main.assertion.1\] .* s.a written: FAILURE$
^\[main.assertion.2\] .* s.b not written: SUCCESS$
^\[main.assertion.3\] .* g.a written: FAILURE$
^\[main.assertion.4\] .* g.b not written: SUCCESS$
^\[main.assertion.5\] .* t.in.x written: FAILURE$
^\[main.assertion.6\] .* t.a not written: SUCCESS$
^\[main.assertion.7\] .* u.arr\[0\] written: FAILURE$
^\[main.assertion.8\] .* u.b not written: SUCCESS$
^\[main.assertion.9\] .* v\[1\].a written: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
^\[main\.assigns\.\d+\] .*: FAILURE$
^\[main\.loop_assigns\.\d+\] .*: FAILURE$
--
The loop assigns inference replaces the bodies of the functions that DFCC does
not instrument by summaries of their writes. A summary writes the members that
the function writes, of globals and of the objects its parameters point to,
rather than those whole objects, so that the loop does not havoc the members
the function does not write. A write to a part of a member writes the whole
member (here, `t.in` and `u.arr`), and a write through a parameter that the
function changes writes the whole object it may point to.
